 "rubric": "Thesis topic: \"A Legally Grounded Empirical Evaluation of Explainable AI for Consumer Credit Scoring\".\n\nLabel each paper from its title and abstract ONLY (no outside knowledge):\nRELEVANT      - directly studies at least one central facet: (a) ML/statistical models for consumer credit scoring, credit risk or loan default; (b) explainability/interpretability methods applied to credit/lending/finance decisions; (c) methods or metrics for evaluating explanation quality (faithfulness, stability, etc.) of post-hoc explainers (SHAP, LIME, counterfactuals) on tabular models; (d) legal/regulatory explanation, transparency or fairness requirements for automated credit or profiling decisions (GDPR Art. 22, AI Act, consumer credit law); (e) fairness/discrimination in credit/lending algorithms.\nUNCERTAIN     - plausibly useful but peripheral or ambiguous (e.g. generic XAI survey, XAI in a non-credit financial task, generic algorithmic-fairness method).\nNOT_RELEVANT  - a different domain where the overlap is incidental (e.g. SHAP used in medicine, bias in clinical studies, non-credit finance without XAI).\n",
